(point 1), and the tiger eats the dog (point 2), we can apply the rule "If something eats the tiger, then it eats the dog" (point 3).\n- Therefore, the mouse, which eats the tiger, must also eat the dog.\n\nHowever, we need to ensure that there are no contradictions or missing pieces of information that might affect this conclusion. Let\'s review the other relevant information:.\n- The mouse does not see the tiger (point 4).\n- The tiger is nice (point 5).\n- The tiger is green and big (point 6).\n\nThese additional details do not contradict our conclusion. The tiger being green and big (point 6) does not affect the logical chain of eating relationships. The tiger being nice (point 5) is a separate statement about the tiger\'s disposition and does not impact the eating relationships.\n\nGiven all the information, the logical conclusion is that the mouse eats the dog because the mouse eats the tiger, and the tiger eats the dog.\n\nTherefore, the statement "The mouse eats the dog" is true based on the given context.\n\n</think>.\n<answer>. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
