o Fiona since she is blue, not red.).\n     - If Anne is blue and Anne is smart then Anne is young. (This doesn\'t apply to Fiona since she is blue, not red.).\n     - All smart, young things are green. (This doesn\'t apply to Fiona since she is blue, not red.).\n     - If something is red and big, it is young. (This doesn\'t apply to Fiona since she is blue, not red.).\n     - If something is big, it is young. (This doesn\'t apply to Fiona since she is blue, not red.).\n\n5. **If something is big and young then it is smart.**.\n   - This doesn\'t apply to Fiona since she is blue, not red.\n\n6. **If something is green then it is cold.**.\n   - This doesn\'t apply to Fiona since she is blue, not red.\n\nFrom the information provided, we can see that:.\n- Fiona is blue and red, but we don\'t have information about her age.\n- Fiona is smart, but we don\'t have information about her age.\n- We know that if something is big and young, it is smart, but we don\'t have information about Erin\'s age.\n- We know that if something is big, it is young, but we don\'t have information about Erin\'s age.\n\nSince we don\'t have any information about Erin\'s age or color, we cannot definitively determine if Erin is green or not. Therefore, the statement "Erin is not green" is unknown based on the given information.\n\n<｜Assistant｜> <answer>. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
