.**.\n15. **If something is young then it is red.**.\n16. **If something eats the dog and it is red then the dog is young.**.\n\nNow, let\'s focus on the statement "The tiger does not see the dog.".\n\n- From point 6, we know the tiger eats the cow.\n- From point 11, if something sees the tiger, it needs the bald eagle. Since the tiger needs the bald eagle (point 7), it means the tiger needs the bald eagle.\n- From point 14, if something is red, it eats the tiger. Since the tiger needs the bald eagle (point 7), and the tiger is red (point 14), it means the tiger eats the bald eagle.\n- From point 16, if something eats the dog and it is red, the dog is young. Since the dog eats the bald eagle (point 5), and the dog is red (point 14), the dog is young.\n\nHowever, we need to determine if the tiger sees the dog. Let\'s consider the following:.\n\n- From point 11, if something sees the tiger, it needs the bald eagle. Since the tiger needs the bald eagle (point 7), it means the tiger needs the bald eagle.\n- From point 13, if something sees the tiger, it needs the bald eagle. Since the tiger needs the bald eagle (point 7), it means the tiger needs the bald eagle.\n\nSince the tiger needs the bald eagle and the tiger sees the bald eagle (point 4), the tiger needs the bald eagle. Therefore, the tiger does not see the dog.\n\nThus, the statement "The tiger does not see the dog" is true based on the given information.\n\n</think>.\n<answer>. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
